1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,600
It's everyone's favorite.

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:07,600
Big time, small time, anytime.

3
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:11,340
Whether it's vanilla, chocolate, or whatever.

4
00:00:11,560 --> 00:00:12,600
We need some M&Ms.

5
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:13,540
How about brownies?

6
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:14,480
Sure, all right, why not?

7
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,140
We've got the scoop on this centuries-old dessert

8
00:00:17,141 --> 00:00:18,980
in all of its flavorful forms.

9
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:20,740
That's good.

10
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:22,120
That's a 10.

11
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:24,700
From the cow to the cone,

12
00:00:25,420 --> 00:00:29,100
it's the untold story of ice cream on Modern Marvels.

13
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Welcome to a world like none you've ever seen.

14
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:51,140
A magical land of ice cream floats, chocolate waterfalls,

15
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:53,440
and frozen fantasies.

16
00:00:54,880 --> 00:00:57,700
Charlie and the Chocolate Factory ain't got nothing on us.

17
00:00:57,701 --> 00:01:02,140
This is the Dreyer's Plant in Bakersfield, California,

18
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:05,040
the world's largest and busiest ice cream factory.

19
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:11,040
Each year, the 28 production lines churn out 90 different

20
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:16,660
ice cream products, totaling over 100 million gallons.

21
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:21,760
That helps satisfy the world's 4 billion gallon a year appetite.

22
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:25,300
Not surprisingly, one of the leaders is the good old USA,

23
00:01:25,301 --> 00:01:29,700
USA, where the average person downs 23.2 quarts a year.

24
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:35,040
We're all kids when it comes to ice cream.

25
00:01:35,440 --> 00:01:36,460
We all love it.

26
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:37,640
You know, it's comfort food.

27
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:38,940
You've had a bad day?

28
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:40,720
Have a bowl of ice cream.

29
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:42,220
You've had a good day?

30
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:44,140
Celebrate with a bowl of ice cream.

31
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:51,220
Today, ice cream production is a sophisticated and technologically advanced science.

32
00:01:51,221 --> 00:01:56,380
But the same basic principles from hundreds of years ago still apply.

33
00:01:57,560 --> 00:02:02,980
The way we make ice cream in the manufacturing scale is the same way that you would make ice cream at home in a hand-turned freezer.

34
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:08,380
The differences are, obviously, we do it in scale in a continuous process rather than a batch process at home.

35
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:15,600
The process begins with ice cream's most important ingredient, milk.

36
00:02:15,601 --> 00:02:22,400
The total liquid supply of this facility is about a half a million gallons.

37
00:02:22,401 --> 00:02:27,840
So we take those basic ingredients of milk, condensed milk, and cream, coupled with sugars,

38
00:02:27,841 --> 00:02:29,480
and that makes the base mix.

39
00:02:29,481 --> 00:02:36,280
We'll also add in cocoa powders and stabilizers into the mix room.

40
00:02:36,281 --> 00:02:41,640
Once the mix has been blended, it's piped to the pasteurizer, where it's heated to 179

41
00:02:41,641 --> 00:02:46,380
for 25 seconds to kill bacteria.

42
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:49,780
Then it's homogenized, forced through tiny valves

43
00:02:49,880 --> 00:02:53,280
at a pressure of 4,000 pounds per square inch

44
00:02:53,380 --> 00:02:57,280
to break down and disperse the fat globules.

45
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:58,900
Just like putting your thumb over a hose,

46
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,200
it aspirates that mix and creates a very consistent

47
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:05,240
or homogeneous product.

48
00:03:05,340 --> 00:03:07,740
The product is then cooled to 40 degrees

49
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:11,640
before being pumped into the various flavor tanks.

50
00:03:11,641 --> 00:03:15,020
We'll add in the cherry flavors or the strawberry flavor.

51
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,320
And as we get into the freezer, now is where we begin to create the ice cream.

52
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:23,480
The flavored mix is continuously pumped into the freezer

53
00:03:23,481 --> 00:03:28,960
and enters a long, cold cylinder that is jacketed by a liquid ammonia freezing agent.

54
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:33,440
The temperature of the mix drops to 21 to 23 degrees.

55
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:36,880
As the mix freezes to the walls of the cylinder,

56
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:40,640
ice crystals are scraped off by the revolving blades of a paddle

57
00:03:40,641 --> 00:03:41,880
called a dasher.

58
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:45,320
The blades have an additional purpose,

59
00:03:45,980 --> 00:03:47,140
whipping air into the mix,

60
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:50,120
creating the frozen foam we know as ice cream.

61
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:54,580
One of the largest ingredients inside ice cream

62
00:03:54,581 --> 00:03:56,180
that most people don't know is actually air.

63
00:03:56,260 --> 00:03:57,120
We call it overrun.

64
00:03:57,660 --> 00:04:00,580
Premium products are required to have 100% overrun,

65
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:03,160
so for every one cup of mix, we add one cup of air.

66
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,980
In the U.S., ice cream must, by definition,

67
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,000
contain at least 10% butterfat.

68
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:14,460
When it's pumped out of the freezer, it has the consistency of soft serve.

69
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:16,900
But that won't last for long.

70
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:23,640
The cartons next head into the blast hardener, kept at a bone-chilling 65 degrees below zero.

71
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:28,640
Within hours, the ice cream's temperature will drop to zero degrees Fahrenheit.

72
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:35,780
A blast hardener is really intended to draw all that heat out of it as fast as we can,

73
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:38,660
because the colder we can get it, the faster we can get it,

74
00:04:38,661 --> 00:04:42,740
the less probability we have of creating ice crystals in the product.

75
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:46,900
And ice crystals are really what create that grittiness feel in your mouth.

76
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:50,580
So ice cream manufacturing is all about time and temperature.

77
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:53,040
Not to mention taste.

78
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:59,700
And no one knows that better than Dreyer's official taste tester, John Harrison.

79
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:05,180
A man whose tongue is so sensitive, it's been insured for a million dollars.

80
00:05:05,181 --> 00:05:10,520
What I look for in tasting ice cream is three things.

81
00:05:10,521 --> 00:05:15,660
Now, we all initially eat with our eyes, so it must look appetizing.

82
00:05:15,661 --> 00:05:19,300
Number two is the flavor and flavor balance.

83
00:05:19,301 --> 00:05:21,680
And thirdly is the body and texture.

84
00:05:21,681 --> 00:05:27,300
John's job is to make sure that each carton lives up to its premium label, in terms of

85
00:05:27,301 --> 00:05:28,940
taste and presentation.

86
00:05:28,941 --> 00:05:31,500
Sort of like a wine taster.

87
00:05:31,501 --> 00:05:36,980
I start with the white wines of ice cream, vanilla, French vanilla, vanilla bean, double vanilla,

88
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:43,680
and I work my way up to the heavy Bordeaux's of ice cream, such as black walnut, mint chocolate chip.

89
00:05:45,140 --> 00:05:50,140
When tasting ice cream, John uses a gold-plated spoon that doesn't leave an aftertaste.

90
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:51,980
Pure vanilla.

91
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:57,780
My tasting method is the three S's, which stands for swirl, smack, and spit.

92
00:05:57,781 --> 00:05:58,780
Mmm.

93
00:05:58,781 --> 00:05:59,780
Whoo!

94
00:05:59,781 --> 00:06:00,780
That's good.

95
00:06:00,781 --> 00:06:01,780
Cougars and cream.

96
00:06:01,781 --> 00:06:06,780
You don't have to swallow to taste.

97
00:06:06,781 --> 00:06:08,780
We swallow to get nutrition.

98
00:06:08,781 --> 00:06:14,780
You get the cream, the cookies, vanilla, sweetness.

99
00:06:14,781 --> 00:06:19,780
As you can see, I've been swallowing more than I should.

100
00:06:19,781 --> 00:06:22,780
It's so good, though.

101
00:06:22,781 --> 00:06:26,780
The ice cream John's tasting is very different from the ice cream.

102
00:06:26,781 --> 00:06:30,780
is very different from the world's first ice cream.

103
00:06:30,781 --> 00:06:33,780
I wish I could tell you ice cream was invented on this day

104
00:06:33,781 --> 00:06:35,780
with this person, but the fact is,

105
00:06:35,781 --> 00:06:39,780
we don't really know where ice cream was invented or when.

106
00:06:39,781 --> 00:06:44,780
We do know that there's lots of myths and legends around ice cream.

107
00:06:44,781 --> 00:06:47,780
Many believe the Emperor Nero would send slaves into the mountains

108
00:06:47,781 --> 00:06:51,780
to bring back snow and ice for snow ice cream,

109
00:06:51,781 --> 00:06:54,780
made with wine, fruit, and honey.

110
00:06:54,781 --> 00:06:57,740
Another legend has Marco Polo returning from China

111
00:06:57,741 --> 00:07:00,100
with a recipe for a sherbet-like dessert.

112
00:07:01,460 --> 00:07:03,800
Ice cream as we know and love it today

113
00:07:03,801 --> 00:07:05,620
really has its roots in Italy,

114
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:07,460
where they call their ice cream gelato.

115
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:10,760
The Italians really perfected ice cream

116
00:07:10,761 --> 00:07:13,660
in their cafes, in high society.

117
00:07:14,340 --> 00:07:16,040
We can thank the Italians for that.

118
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:20,740
Compared to American ice cream,

119
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:23,900
gelato contains less fat and less added air.

120
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:27,180
As a result, it has a rich, creamier taste.

121
00:07:27,181 --> 00:07:28,780
Mmm, tasty.

122
00:07:28,781 --> 00:07:30,580
Fussy.

123
00:07:30,581 --> 00:07:32,480
Gelato varies from region to region,

124
00:07:32,481 --> 00:07:35,620
and even within a city, you can have something very different.

125
00:07:35,621 --> 00:07:37,220
It's like rose water.

126
00:07:37,221 --> 00:07:41,020
But the Italians do tend to use more milk and less cream

127
00:07:41,021 --> 00:07:44,140
than American-style ice cream.

128
00:07:44,141 --> 00:07:47,840
John Snyder is America's gelato guru.

129
00:07:47,841 --> 00:07:52,380
At Il Laboratorio del Gelato on New York's Lower East Side,

130
00:07:52,381 --> 00:07:54,240
he creates this centuries-old dessert

131
00:07:54,241 --> 00:07:57,040
for more than 200 of Manhattan's finest restaurants.

132
00:07:59,420 --> 00:08:01,480
Today we're going to be making espresso gelato,

133
00:08:01,880 --> 00:08:05,020
and we begin with just pure espresso beans.

134
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:10,120
We use about a gallon of espresso beans for one batch of gelato,

135
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:13,360
and this goes right in the blender with the beans.

136
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:16,440
Next, John adds two gallons of a base mix,

137
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:18,460
consisting of milk, cream, and sugar.

138
00:08:19,060 --> 00:08:22,160
He then blends the mixture and heats it,

139
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:24,080
infusing the beans into the mix.

140
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:27,860
and just let this come to almost a boil, but not quite.

141
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:33,200
An hour later, it's time to strain out the beans.

142
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:40,020
John then adds a little fresh brewed coffee to the bean and milk concoction

143
00:08:40,021 --> 00:08:43,400
and pours it into a gelato machine for churning and freezing.

144
00:08:45,660 --> 00:08:48,740
Three additional gallons of base mix round out the recipe.

145
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:54,020
Well, the recipes that I've created are based on years of a lot of tasting,

146
00:08:54,240 --> 00:08:56,100
my travels all over the world.

147
00:08:57,100 --> 00:08:59,280
We try and make just the very best

148
00:08:59,281 --> 00:09:01,440
and also the very purest.

149
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:04,080
And I can start taking it out.

150
00:09:04,820 --> 00:09:07,480
20 minutes later, it's gelato time.

151
00:09:08,660 --> 00:09:10,920
This gelato will give you a pretty good jolt.

152
00:09:14,820 --> 00:09:15,520
Espresso.

153
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:17,480
Best way to start your day.

154
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:21,620
It's doubtful whether America's founding fathers

155
00:09:21,621 --> 00:09:23,860
started their days off with espresso gelato.

156
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:26,080
But they did enjoy their ice cream.

157
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:28,820
George Washington was a fan.

158
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:31,580
Thomas Jefferson had a weakness for vanilla.

159
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:35,620
And Dolly Madison served it at her husband's second inauguration.

160
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:42,980
But it was the invention of the salt and ice hand-cranked batch freezer in 1846

161
00:09:42,981 --> 00:09:46,900
by housewife Nancy Johnson that turned ice cream

162
00:09:46,901 --> 00:09:49,540
into a household food that everyone could enjoy.

163
00:09:49,541 --> 00:09:54,540
That old homemade method is still revered at Penn State

164
00:09:54,541 --> 00:09:56,540
University, the country's leading authority

165
00:09:56,541 --> 00:09:58,540
on the making of ice cream.

166
00:09:58,541 --> 00:10:02,540
We'll use this demonstration with the salt and ice freezer

167
00:10:02,541 --> 00:10:05,540
to demonstrate the basic principles that underlie freezing

168
00:10:05,541 --> 00:10:08,540
of ice cream mix into ice cream.

169
00:10:08,541 --> 00:10:14,540
The freezing cylinder will be charged to be roughly half full

170
00:10:14,541 --> 00:10:16,540
of ice cream mix.

171
00:10:16,541 --> 00:10:20,800
Then the scraping mechanism is inserted into the freezing system.

172
00:10:22,660 --> 00:10:29,720
Now this freezer is very similar to the freezer that Nancy Johnson developed in 1846,

173
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:33,880
except that we have an electric motor rather than a hand crank.

174
00:10:35,540 --> 00:10:38,320
Salt and ice are added to act as the freezing agent.

175
00:10:38,980 --> 00:10:42,420
They form a brine solution, which has a very low freezing point.

176
00:10:42,421 --> 00:10:45,420
As the brine absorbs heat from the ice cream mix

177
00:10:45,421 --> 00:10:49,420
and the cylinder, the mix itself begins to freeze.

178
00:10:49,421 --> 00:10:51,420
And you'll notice that in this system,

179
00:10:51,421 --> 00:10:53,420
the scraper mechanism is fixed,

180
00:10:53,421 --> 00:10:57,420
and the cylinder rotates around the scraper mechanism here.

181
00:10:57,421 --> 00:11:02,420
45 minutes later, the ice cream is ready.

182
00:11:02,421 --> 00:11:05,420
Oh, and you can see that this is whipped up nicely.

183
00:11:05,421 --> 00:11:09,420
If you look carefully, you can see little balls,

184
00:11:09,421 --> 00:11:16,100
little balls and you'll see this kind of stringiness a part of this is due to the ice and part of this is also

185
00:11:16,101 --> 00:11:18,920
Due to destabilized fat that's in the ice cream

186
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:23,660
Because of the long slow churning process, but this is truly homemade ice cream

187
00:11:25,740 --> 00:11:29,600
While most of today's ice cream makers have abandoned the old-fashioned methods

188
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:34,520
Tradition lives on at Grater's a family-run business in Cincinnati

189
00:11:34,521 --> 00:11:41,600
We are the only ice cream company, I think, anywhere in the world that uses the French

190
00:11:41,601 --> 00:11:43,280
pot method of making ice cream.

191
00:11:43,281 --> 00:11:48,620
If you Google French pot, you'll find us and nobody else.

192
00:11:48,621 --> 00:11:52,500
Grater's is a fourth generation company that has been making ice cream the same basic

193
00:11:52,501 --> 00:11:58,260
way for more than 130 years.

194
00:11:58,261 --> 00:12:02,760
The process begins with pouring two gallons of ice cream mix into one of Grater's unique

195
00:12:02,761 --> 00:12:05,760
French pots.

196
00:12:05,761 --> 00:12:09,000
This is a very similar ice cream freezer to what we started

197
00:12:09,001 --> 00:12:10,960
with in 1870.

198
00:12:10,961 --> 00:12:13,680
It's been modernized slightly, mechanicalized in modern

199
00:12:13,681 --> 00:12:15,100
refrigeration.

200
00:12:15,101 --> 00:12:17,740
What it consists of is a five-gallon stainless steel

201
00:12:17,741 --> 00:12:22,780
drum submerged in calcium chloride.

202
00:12:22,781 --> 00:12:25,620
As the pot slowly spins, the mix begins

203
00:12:25,621 --> 00:12:28,280
to freeze against its sides.

204
00:12:28,281 --> 00:12:31,460
A blade softly scrapes the sides, folding the ice cream

205
00:12:31,461 --> 00:12:32,241
into itself.

206
00:12:32,242 --> 00:12:37,380
This folding process prevents air from whipping into the ice cream and accounts

207
00:12:37,381 --> 00:12:40,871
for its thick and creamy consistency.

208
00:12:40,872 --> 00:12:45,400
The reason no one else uses the French pot today

209
00:12:45,401 --> 00:12:47,176
is that it's so labor-intensive.

210
00:12:47,177 --> 00:12:50,340
You can only make two gallons per machine, and modern ice

211
00:12:50,341 --> 00:12:56,342
cream machines, even the small ones, will make ten gallons in ten minutes.

212
00:12:56,343 --> 00:12:57,640
After 15 minutes

213
00:12:57,641 --> 00:13:00,570
of spinning, the ice cream is ready to be packed.

214
00:13:00,571 --> 00:13:03,560
But it's so dense that no equipment can handle it.

215
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:09,237
Where all of our pints are hand-packed, there is no automation back there.

216
00:13:09,238 --> 00:13:10,120
Our packing machine

217
00:13:10,121 --> 00:13:15,800
is named Bud. We pack about 6,000 pints that way on a daily basis.

218
00:13:18,520 --> 00:13:21,560
Where a typical pint of ice cream may weigh as little as eight ounces,

219
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:24,600
a grater's pint weighs nearly a full pound.

220
00:13:24,601 --> 00:13:31,880
The unique thing about Grader's ice cream is it is just like what your great-grandparents

221
00:13:31,881 --> 00:13:34,180
would have had over a hundred years ago.

222
00:13:34,181 --> 00:13:38,280
We are literally the only people in the world that continue to use the French pot process

223
00:13:38,281 --> 00:13:43,960
to make ice cream, and we're still making it just two gallons at a time.

224
00:13:43,961 --> 00:13:48,820
By the late 1800s, people were getting their ice cream at mom and pop soda fountains, like

225
00:13:48,821 --> 00:13:49,820
this one.

226
00:13:49,821 --> 00:13:52,120
Aglamisus Brothers in Cincinnati.

227
00:13:52,121 --> 00:13:59,000
When you walk into a nostalgic ice cream parlor like this, you're really coming back

228
00:13:59,001 --> 00:14:04,680
to a place that doesn't really exist anymore on this planet.

229
00:14:04,681 --> 00:14:10,460
This shop is only one of a handful of soda fountains that remains from the period.

230
00:14:10,461 --> 00:14:14,240
This is the authentic ice cream fountain that we've had since this building was built in

231
00:14:14,241 --> 00:14:17,300
1913.

232
00:14:17,301 --> 00:14:24,540
Our formulas are actually the same as they were many years ago in respect to cream, honey,

233
00:14:24,541 --> 00:14:26,940
eggs and sugar in our product.

234
00:14:26,941 --> 00:14:28,740
What flavor do you like?

235
00:14:28,741 --> 00:14:32,760
The late 1800s ushered in an era of ice cream innovations.

236
00:14:32,761 --> 00:14:39,300
Among them, a colorful combination that included ice cream, flavored toppings and whipped cream,

237
00:14:39,301 --> 00:14:42,820
the ice cream sundae.

238
00:14:42,821 --> 00:14:46,700
More than half a dozen communities claim to be its birthplace.

239
00:14:46,701 --> 00:14:50,000
from Two Rivers, Wisconsin, where a historical marker

240
00:14:50,100 --> 00:14:52,400
commemorates the event.

241
00:14:52,500 --> 00:14:55,200
The community has always taken great pride in the history

242
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:57,240
of being home of the ice cream sundae,

243
00:14:57,340 --> 00:15:00,140
invented by one Ed Berners at his ice cream parlor

244
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:05,960
in the Franklin House building in 1881.

245
00:15:06,060 --> 00:15:08,360
To Ithaca, New York.

246
00:15:08,460 --> 00:15:10,460
The story that's claimed in Ithaca

247
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:14,700
is that the sundae was invented in 1892

248
00:15:14,701 --> 00:15:17,360
at Colt and Platt's Pharmacy.

249
00:15:17,361 --> 00:15:20,100
There is no documentation for the Burner story

250
00:15:20,101 --> 00:15:23,340
and several pieces of documentation for the Ithaca story.

251
00:15:24,500 --> 00:15:26,640
To Evanston, Illinois.

252
00:15:26,641 --> 00:15:29,840
One pharmacist in Evanston, according to legend,

253
00:15:29,841 --> 00:15:34,460
decided to serve ice cream with syrup on Sunday

254
00:15:34,461 --> 00:15:36,360
as a way of driving sales.

255
00:15:36,361 --> 00:15:38,500
And out of respect for the Lord's name,

256
00:15:38,501 --> 00:15:42,260
he changed the spelling from D-A-Y to D-A-E.

257
00:15:42,261 --> 00:15:45,760
We may never know who really invented the ice cream sundae,

258
00:15:45,761 --> 00:15:49,260
but we do know every sundae needs a dish.

259
00:15:49,261 --> 00:15:51,260
And if you don't have a dish,

260
00:15:51,261 --> 00:15:55,260
you'd better have ice cream's most trusted companion, the cone.

261
00:15:59,260 --> 00:16:01,260
The biggest ice cream sundae in the world

262
00:16:01,261 --> 00:16:05,260
was made in Alberta, Canada in 1988.

263
00:16:05,261 --> 00:16:07,260
It weighed over 24 tons,

264
00:16:07,261 --> 00:16:11,260
including over 4 tons of syrup and toppings.

265
00:16:11,261 --> 00:16:14,600
Ice cream will return on Modern Marvels,

266
00:16:14,601 --> 00:16:16,260
here on History.

267
00:16:20,260 --> 00:16:24,600
For hundreds of years, ice cream was content to go it alone,

268
00:16:24,601 --> 00:16:27,600
served up in dishes, bowls, and glasses.

269
00:16:27,601 --> 00:16:30,020
But since the turn of the 20th century,

270
00:16:30,021 --> 00:16:33,220
it's been married to the perfect partner.

271
00:16:33,221 --> 00:16:37,220
That would be the cone, a wonderful little invention

272
00:16:37,221 --> 00:16:39,720
with but one purpose.

273
00:16:39,721 --> 00:16:43,720
Cones are really a vehicle just to eat ice cream.

274
00:16:43,721 --> 00:16:47,720
So you don't buy cones unless you're buying ice cream.

275
00:16:47,721 --> 00:16:49,720
And by the looks of things,

276
00:16:49,721 --> 00:16:52,720
people are buying a whole lot of ice cream.

277
00:16:52,721 --> 00:16:55,720
This is the Joy Cone Company in Hermitage, Pennsylvania,

278
00:16:55,721 --> 00:16:59,720
the world's largest ice cream cone plant.

279
00:16:59,721 --> 00:17:04,720
Every year, Joy Cone turns out a staggering 1.5 billion cones.

280
00:17:04,721 --> 00:17:08,720
That's over 5.5 million cones every day.

281
00:17:09,720 --> 00:17:13,720
Amazing when you consider the genesis of the first cone.

282
00:17:13,721 --> 00:17:16,720
There's a couple of different competing theories,

283
00:17:16,721 --> 00:17:20,720
but the one that we adhere to is in the 1904 World's Fair.

284
00:17:20,721 --> 00:17:23,720
It was in St. Louis, and there was two vendors.

285
00:17:23,721 --> 00:17:26,720
One was making waffles, and the person next to him

286
00:17:26,721 --> 00:17:29,720
was selling scoops of ice cream in a dish.

287
00:17:29,721 --> 00:17:31,720
Well, the ice cream vendor ran out of dishes,

288
00:17:31,721 --> 00:17:36,720
and so the waffle cone vendor folded the waffle into a conical shape.

289
00:17:36,721 --> 00:17:40,560
And so they started serving the ice cream inside the waffle,

290
00:17:40,660 --> 00:17:44,520
and it was developed as the waffle cone.

291
00:17:44,620 --> 00:17:47,960
Waffle cones are still being made at Joy-Con,

292
00:17:48,060 --> 00:17:54,380
as are sugar cones and the crispy, flat-bottomed cake cones.

293
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:58,080
A cake cone is made with some basic ingredients,

294
00:17:58,180 --> 00:18:04,480
flour, tapioca flour, water, sugar, and then minor ingredients.

295
00:18:04,481 --> 00:18:08,280
The ingredients are dumped into a mixer, cooled,

296
00:18:08,380 --> 00:18:10,960
and then pumped as a liquid batter into the ovens.

297
00:18:13,020 --> 00:18:15,420
With the K-cones, it's a molded product.

298
00:18:15,520 --> 00:18:18,400
Batter is formed into the mold, and then a plug

299
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:21,200
drops into the mold, so the batter forms

300
00:18:21,300 --> 00:18:23,700
between the cavity and the plug.

301
00:18:23,800 --> 00:18:25,200
It goes in liquid.

302
00:18:25,300 --> 00:18:29,280
90 seconds later, it'll drop out as a fully formed cone.

303
00:18:29,380 --> 00:18:32,800
And as you can see, we're making color cups today.

304
00:18:32,801 --> 00:18:36,300
The cones come out of the oven at about 350 degrees,

305
00:18:36,301 --> 00:18:38,800
and they come down this conveyor to cool off

306
00:18:38,801 --> 00:18:40,800
before they're packed onto the conveyor.

307
00:18:40,801 --> 00:18:45,800
This oven is going to generate over 120,000 cones in a single day,

308
00:18:45,801 --> 00:18:50,800
and the whole line over 700,000 cones in one day.

309
00:18:50,801 --> 00:18:53,800
Quality control is never a problem at Joy-Con,

310
00:18:53,801 --> 00:18:56,800
with CEO Joe George working the floor.

311
00:18:56,801 --> 00:19:01,800
You want it to be well-baked without being burnt in any spot.

312
00:19:01,801 --> 00:19:07,400
in any spot, and you want it to be soft in texture

313
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:12,920
without being too weak, and it's got to taste good.

314
00:19:13,020 --> 00:19:14,220
And this one passes a test.

315
00:19:18,420 --> 00:19:22,080
Unlike the molded cake cones, sugar cones and waffle cones

316
00:19:22,180 --> 00:19:26,220
are rolled products, baked flat in the oven.

317
00:19:26,320 --> 00:19:28,100
Once they're warm and malleable, they're

318
00:19:28,200 --> 00:19:31,800
dropped upon a spinner that forms the actual cone shape.

319
00:19:31,801 --> 00:19:39,260
This is our sugar cone machine, which produces 10,000 cones an hour, or 240,000 cones a day.

320
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:42,720
When we make all these cones, we want to make sure it's excellent taste.

321
00:19:43,080 --> 00:19:46,240
But we also need to make sure it's a nice, sealed tip,

322
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:49,800
because we want to make sure that you're eating the ice cream and not wearing it.

323
00:19:51,420 --> 00:19:52,420
Now you try it.

324
00:19:53,700 --> 00:19:58,180
The folks at Baskin-Robbins have turned filling ice cream cones into an art form.

325
00:19:58,181 --> 00:20:00,680
All workers are trained in the proper technique

326
00:20:00,681 --> 00:20:02,680
for making the perfect four-ounce scoop.

327
00:20:02,681 --> 00:20:04,180
Not too bad.

328
00:20:04,181 --> 00:20:06,180
One of our Baskin-Robbins franchisees,

329
00:20:06,181 --> 00:20:09,180
Mitch Cohn from New York, holds the Guinness Book of World Records

330
00:20:09,181 --> 00:20:14,180
for the most scoops successfully executed and applied to a cone in one minute.

331
00:20:14,181 --> 00:20:16,180
He did 19 of them.

332
00:20:16,181 --> 00:20:20,180
Now we're going to make the perfect scoop using the Baskin-Robbins

333
00:20:20,181 --> 00:20:21,180
custom ice cream scoop.

334
00:20:21,181 --> 00:20:26,180
Want to get in there, get a big chunk, get the ice cream to roll around inside the scoop,

335
00:20:26,181 --> 00:20:33,780
around inside the scoop make a second pass at it to tighten it up bring it back apply it firmly

336
00:20:33,781 --> 00:20:37,860
but gently to this cone and there you go perfect ice cream scoop

337
00:20:40,420 --> 00:20:43,300
and how many licks does it take to put away a scoop of ice cream

338
00:20:44,580 --> 00:20:48,020
who better to ask than john harrison the man with a million dollar tongue

339
00:20:48,021 --> 00:20:56,800
A typical cone, one scoop, looking at four fluid ounces, it takes about 50 licks to polish

340
00:20:56,801 --> 00:21:02,400
off that scoop of ice cream.

341
00:21:02,401 --> 00:21:04,200
We decided to put that number to the test.

342
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:25,860
This lucky lady needed 100 licks and a half dozen bites.

343
00:21:27,920 --> 00:21:31,680
But ice cream consumption can have its headaches, literally.

344
00:21:32,300 --> 00:21:35,380
At Penn State University, we learned about the most common,

345
00:21:36,060 --> 00:21:37,520
the dreaded brain freeze.

346
00:21:39,740 --> 00:21:45,520
After the ice cream is taken in and comes in contact with the upper palate of the mouth,

347
00:21:45,521 --> 00:21:47,380
it constricts these blood vessels.

348
00:21:48,020 --> 00:21:50,420
and it restricts the flow of blood.

349
00:21:50,421 --> 00:21:54,120
A few seconds later, the palate is rewarmed,

350
00:21:54,121 --> 00:21:56,460
and these blood vessels dilate,

351
00:21:56,461 --> 00:21:58,260
and the blood returns to normal.

352
00:21:58,261 --> 00:22:01,460
At this point, the blood rush back to the palate

353
00:22:01,461 --> 00:22:04,100
is rather significant, and the nerve endings

354
00:22:04,101 --> 00:22:07,140
in the upper roof of the mouth sense this as pain.

355
00:22:07,141 --> 00:22:09,580
So it's not the ingestion of the cold ice cream,

356
00:22:09,581 --> 00:22:11,980
it's actually the return of the warmth

357
00:22:11,981 --> 00:22:13,940
after the ice cream is swallowed.

358
00:22:13,941 --> 00:22:16,220
And this pain then is transferred to the forehead

359
00:22:16,221 --> 00:22:19,220
as a very severe but brief headache.

360
00:22:19,221 --> 00:22:24,220
And the cure for the freeze?

361
00:22:24,221 --> 00:22:27,220
I would recommend taking smaller portions at a time,

362
00:22:27,221 --> 00:22:30,220
or licking smaller portions of ice cream off the cone,

363
00:22:30,221 --> 00:22:32,220
or eating it from the dish.

364
00:22:32,221 --> 00:22:35,220
But you knew that, didn't you?

365
00:22:35,221 --> 00:22:40,220
Of course the cone isn't the only way to eat ice cream with one hand.

366
00:22:40,221 --> 00:22:43,220
The ice cream bar was invented in the 1920s

367
00:22:43,221 --> 00:22:46,820
when a young boy came into a confectioner's shop in Iowa

368
00:22:46,920 --> 00:22:48,820
by the name of Christian Nelson.

369
00:22:48,920 --> 00:22:52,120
And the boy only had a nickel, and he couldn't decide

370
00:22:52,220 --> 00:22:55,060
between buying ice cream and buying chocolate.

371
00:22:55,160 --> 00:22:56,660
And he wanted to have both.

372
00:22:56,760 --> 00:22:58,560
And that got Christian Nelson to thinking,

373
00:22:58,660 --> 00:23:00,780
how could I put these two things together

374
00:23:00,880 --> 00:23:02,880
to satisfy this customer?

375
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:09,480
And what he invented became the Eskimo pie as we know it.

376
00:23:09,580 --> 00:23:12,180
Today, the Eskimo pie and its offshoot,

377
00:23:12,181 --> 00:23:15,520
the Eskimo Pie Crunch Bar, are born at the dryer's plant

378
00:23:15,620 --> 00:23:17,580
in Bakersfield.

379
00:23:17,680 --> 00:23:19,720
So this is what we would call a molded product.

380
00:23:19,820 --> 00:23:22,460
And the mold is essentially just like you'd make an ice cube

381
00:23:22,560 --> 00:23:24,760
at home, where you put water into an ice cube tray,

382
00:23:24,860 --> 00:23:28,000
only we do it in a much larger scale.

383
00:23:28,100 --> 00:23:31,660
That means 20,000 bars every hour.

384
00:23:31,760 --> 00:23:33,740
You spray the ice cream inside the mold,

385
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:36,940
and then on the bottom of that, we spray 55 degree below zero

386
00:23:37,040 --> 00:23:39,500
brine solution to actually freeze out the product

387
00:23:39,600 --> 00:23:40,740
inside that mold.

388
00:23:40,741 --> 00:23:43,280
The bar then comes around where it begins to firm up,

389
00:23:43,380 --> 00:23:44,680
and we put a stick inside it.

390
00:23:47,420 --> 00:23:48,980
One more revolution, and the bars

391
00:23:49,080 --> 00:23:51,680
are dumped into a dip tank.

392
00:23:51,780 --> 00:23:53,720
That's where a chocolate and puffed rice coating

393
00:23:53,820 --> 00:23:54,920
is applied to the crunch bar.

394
00:23:58,560 --> 00:24:01,300
Then it's off to be packaged and distributed nationwide.

395
00:24:06,600 --> 00:24:09,260
But ice cream has a soft side too,

396
00:24:09,261 --> 00:24:11,760
And that's an industry unto itself.

397
00:24:11,761 --> 00:24:12,600
Have a nice day.

398
00:24:12,601 --> 00:24:15,600
Thank you very much.

399
00:24:15,601 --> 00:24:17,760
America's five top-selling ice creams

400
00:24:17,761 --> 00:24:21,800
in order of preference are vanilla, chocolate,

401
00:24:21,801 --> 00:24:26,620
Neapolitan, butter pecan, and cookies and cream.

402
00:24:26,621 --> 00:24:29,620
Ice cream will return on Modern Marvels.

403
00:24:34,160 --> 00:24:36,760
Today, ice cream franchises are as much a part

404
00:24:36,761 --> 00:24:40,920
as much a part of our landscape as ice cream itself.

405
00:24:40,921 --> 00:24:42,700
Their roots can be traced to the early days

406
00:24:42,701 --> 00:24:47,400
of another great American institution, the automobile.

407
00:24:47,401 --> 00:24:50,860
As Americans began to use automobiles to get around,

408
00:24:50,861 --> 00:24:54,500
it really transformed the way we enjoyed ice cream.

409
00:24:54,501 --> 00:24:56,660
They started to drive to roadside stands

410
00:24:56,661 --> 00:25:00,400
and get ice cream right from the stands.

411
00:25:00,401 --> 00:25:03,920
Typically, they served soft serve, and that really also

412
00:25:03,921 --> 00:25:05,260
changed the face of ice cream.

413
00:25:06,760 --> 00:25:10,480
Soft serve has less butter fat and less added air than hard ice cream.

414
00:25:11,340 --> 00:25:14,820
And because it's served at a higher temperature, 18 degrees Fahrenheit,

415
00:25:15,200 --> 00:25:19,340
compared to 5 or 10 degrees with hard ice cream, its flavors are more pronounced.

416
00:25:20,160 --> 00:25:23,320
Ice cream being colder, you have less flavor release.

417
00:25:23,420 --> 00:25:25,440
It has a numbing effect on your taste buds,

418
00:25:25,580 --> 00:25:29,240
where at 18 degrees, there's more flavor that comes through.

419
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:34,500
The ice cream stands could pump out soft serve or frozen custard,

420
00:25:35,080 --> 00:25:36,640
a derivative made with egg yolks.

421
00:25:36,760 --> 00:25:41,080
right on the premises. The invention of a revolutionary freezer that could

422
00:25:41,081 --> 00:25:46,520
continuously dispense a semi-frozen dessert made it all possible.

423
00:25:46,521 --> 00:25:51,400
While today's soft serve freezers are far more advanced, they do share many of

424
00:25:51,401 --> 00:25:57,280
the same features. In a typical ice cream freezer, ice cream is added to the

425
00:25:57,281 --> 00:26:01,660
freezing cylinder, it's frozen, it's drawn off, and then it's frozen in the hardening

426
00:26:01,661 --> 00:26:06,360
room, quiescently. In the case of the soft serve ice cream freezer, ice cream is

427
00:26:06,361 --> 00:26:07,320
available on demand.

428
00:26:07,321 --> 00:26:10,120
You get ice cream where all of the freezing has been done

429
00:26:10,121 --> 00:26:14,480
only in the agitated freezing cylinder.

430
00:26:14,481 --> 00:26:17,280
The design of a key part of the soft serve freezer,

431
00:26:17,281 --> 00:26:20,820
the Dasher, is also unique.

432
00:26:20,821 --> 00:26:24,000
You can see these plastic pieces, which

433
00:26:24,001 --> 00:26:26,380
fit over the metal and lock in, are

434
00:26:26,381 --> 00:26:28,200
going to be actually the scraping mechanism.

435
00:26:28,201 --> 00:26:31,760
They'll scrape the ice cream off the walls of the freezer.

436
00:26:31,761 --> 00:26:33,780
You'll also notice this helical design, which

437
00:26:33,781 --> 00:26:36,240
is going to push the ice cream forward,

438
00:26:36,241 --> 00:26:39,080
so that it'll be ready to draw out at any given time.

439
00:26:44,080 --> 00:26:46,720
So now the ice cream's ready.

440
00:26:46,721 --> 00:26:48,720
The soft serve is a little warmer

441
00:26:48,721 --> 00:26:50,360
than the typical hard ice cream,

442
00:26:50,361 --> 00:26:52,080
which makes it eat a little bit better,

443
00:26:52,081 --> 00:26:55,260
so it's soft and refreshing, and it's ready all day long.

444
00:26:56,860 --> 00:26:59,700
One of the first men to see a future in soft serve

445
00:26:59,701 --> 00:27:02,400
was J.F. Grandpa McCullough,

446
00:27:02,401 --> 00:27:05,600
who along with his son Alex created Dairy Queen,

447
00:27:05,601 --> 00:27:08,660
cone with a curl on top.

448
00:27:08,661 --> 00:27:13,100
In their ice cream plant that they owned and operated, they dispensed ice cream in a soft

449
00:27:13,101 --> 00:27:15,600
state out of their freezers on a daily basis.

450
00:27:15,601 --> 00:27:20,540
They wanted to deliver that same product to the consumers.

451
00:27:20,541 --> 00:27:25,900
So they connected with a friend of theirs who had a walk-up stand.

452
00:27:25,901 --> 00:27:28,080
And they did a test run one day.

453
00:27:28,081 --> 00:27:33,540
And in two hours, they served more than 1,600 servings of this ice cream.

454
00:27:33,541 --> 00:27:36,840
So they knew they were on to something.

455
00:27:36,841 --> 00:27:38,340
It didn't take long for Dairy Queen

456
00:27:38,341 --> 00:27:42,280
to become part of American culture.

457
00:27:42,281 --> 00:27:45,780
Dairy Queen tended to go into towns that were smaller,

458
00:27:45,781 --> 00:27:47,880
and it became part of the corner hangout,

459
00:27:47,881 --> 00:27:52,200
where you walked up to get your soft serve cone

460
00:27:52,201 --> 00:27:57,100
and talk to your neighbors and then walk back home.

461
00:27:57,101 --> 00:27:59,760
Today, Galloway Brothers of Neenah, Wisconsin,

462
00:27:59,761 --> 00:28:02,000
is one of several plants that produce the base mix

463
00:28:02,001 --> 00:28:02,710
Dairy Queen.

464
00:28:02,711 --> 00:28:07,680
The ingredients that go into the mix are almost the same as those in hard ice cream,

465
00:28:08,560 --> 00:28:11,680
but the precise formula remains a closely guarded secret.

466
00:28:14,720 --> 00:28:18,270
For Dairy Queen, they have a formula that we must beat.

467
00:28:18,271 --> 00:28:20,400
So much sugar, so much butterfat,

468
00:28:20,401 --> 00:28:21,507
so much milk.

469
00:28:21,508 --> 00:28:28,320
The milk is trucked in daily from local dairies, stored in one of several silos,

470
00:28:28,321 --> 00:28:31,720
and then piped into a blending tank.

471
00:28:31,820 --> 00:28:33,820
This is where we blend all of the ingredients

472
00:28:33,920 --> 00:28:35,260
that go into Dairy Queen.

473
00:28:35,360 --> 00:28:36,660
The computer has a list of ingredients

474
00:28:36,760 --> 00:28:40,400
such as sucrose, corn syrup, cream, condensed skin milk,

475
00:28:40,500 --> 00:28:41,440
and millet.

476
00:28:41,540 --> 00:28:44,700
It blends them based on weight with a Coriolis meter.

477
00:28:44,800 --> 00:28:46,500
The dry ingredients are added up here.

478
00:28:50,140 --> 00:28:53,020
This basically is a great big wearing blender.

479
00:28:53,120 --> 00:28:55,620
We put the sucrose and corn syrup in here,

480
00:28:55,720 --> 00:28:58,320
then add the dry ingredients such as stabilizers

481
00:28:58,321 --> 00:29:03,620
After the mix is blended, it's pumped into a ballast tank,

482
00:29:03,720 --> 00:29:07,460
heated, pasteurized, homogenized, and then cooled.

483
00:29:09,960 --> 00:29:12,460
Behind me is the final operation of the Dairy Queen

484
00:29:12,560 --> 00:29:13,860
manufacturing process.

485
00:29:13,960 --> 00:29:15,680
This is our packaging operation.

486
00:29:15,780 --> 00:29:19,780
Each machine can fill a six-gallon bag in about five seconds.

487
00:29:19,880 --> 00:29:23,980
That's enough to make about 4,000 combs in one minute.

488
00:29:28,320 --> 00:29:31,100
Dairy Queen is the world's largest soft serve franchise,

489
00:29:31,720 --> 00:29:35,000
with over 5,700 locations in North America alone.

490
00:29:35,060 --> 00:29:35,760
Have a nice day.

491
00:29:35,860 --> 00:29:36,560
Thank you very much.

492
00:29:37,020 --> 00:29:39,800
But since the 1970s, the soft serve scene

493
00:29:39,801 --> 00:29:43,240
